WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.960
In my last video, I took a look at a document
which showed some of the math that Terence

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.790
Tao could do when he was seven years old.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.910
But there was one page I didn't show you.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:16.080
And that's this one, a list of some of the
books that Terence had read as a child.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.980
These books are from a longer list of 22,
that Terence had read, by the time he was

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:22.980
eight.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.449
It says that he liked to read entire books,
and that he was keen for advice on what to

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:28.449
read next.

00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:33.570
It also says that he had a remarkable memory
for things that he read.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.280
Terence is now a professor of math at UCLA.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.290
And he's 46.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:40.910
So some of these books are fairly old.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:44.360
But I managed to find some of them at the
library.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.570
I've got Flatland by Edwin Abbott.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:52.310
I've got The Elements of Logic by S Barker.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:55.630
And I've got Calculus Pure and Applied by
Sherlock.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:01:01.190
And I thought, just out of curiosity, I'd
take a look at what these books are like.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:06.149
Before I get stuck into them, I will say these
books contain hard stuff.

00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:08.759
The textbooks are university level.

00:01:08.759 --> 00:01:12.909
And the idea that an eight year old would
read them doesn't even really make sense to

00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:13.909
me.

00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:18.490
But it's true that most of us weren't given
these books as eight year olds, so we will

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.530
never really know if we could have read them.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:27.100
Of course, young Terence was lucky that his
family was able to support his talents.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:33.079
I believe that genius is not just a function
of your mind, but also your environment.

00:01:33.079 --> 00:01:38.340
But that shouldn't take away from how impressive
Terence is.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:44.859
To have the concentration and discipline to
finish these books at any age is awesome.

00:01:44.859 --> 00:01:50.389
Even with the incentive of filming this video,
I have probably spent more time on TikTok

00:01:50.389 --> 00:01:53.909
than I have reading these books over the past
week.

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:58.119
And for that I do look up to the enthusiasm
of an eight year old.

00:01:58.119 --> 00:02:02.159
Flatland is the only one of the books that
I had read already.

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:06.369
And unlike the others, which are textbooks,
this one is fiction.

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:08.920
It's a story about shapes, which I love.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:15.150
I think it's clever, insightful, and it made
me think about dimensions in a way I never

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:16.150
had before.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.770
But honestly, I didn't love everything about
it.

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:24.239
There are some parts that I found hard to
read because of the social commentary about

00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:28.670
gender and class that just doesn't land well
today.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:31.060
The story is set on a two dimensional plane.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:35.709
And the narrator, a self described gentleman,
is a square.

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:39.709
Shapes with more sides are higher in class.

00:02:39.709 --> 00:02:44.560
Hexagons are noble, and something with more
sides than that will approach the priestly

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:45.560
order.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:50.430
Lower class workman such as Square's Butler
are triangles.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:53.160
Woman are a single straight line.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.830
There's his wife right there.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:01.379
Square's house is a Pentagon but this top
down view is not how he sees it.

00:03:01.379 --> 00:03:04.819
To him all things look like lines of varying
size.

00:03:04.819 --> 00:03:09.480
But he can feel the vertices and determine
the angle.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:15.650
Square visits Lineland, which is only one
dimensional, and wishes that he could communicate

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:19.810
to the inhabitants that there is a second
dimension out there.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:26.469
But when Square is visited in Flatland by
a sphere from a third dimension, Square finds

00:03:26.469 --> 00:03:31.900
it hard to accept because he can't imagine
a third dimension.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:38.230
Square wonders if this is true, then what
would come next? and asks Sphere if there

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:40.430
is a fourth dimension.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.469
Sphere says "no, that's ridiculous".

00:03:42.469 --> 00:03:49.739
But really, they're having the same problem
as Square, they simply cannot imagine a dimension

00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:51.460
above their own.

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.819
Overall, it's a worthwhile mind bender.

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:59.819
And it shows that even a book about math can't
be totally separated from the culture in which

00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:01.590
it was written.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:03.249
Moving on to Calculus.

00:04:03.249 --> 00:04:06.629
This is what seems like a fairly standard
calculus textbook.

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:13.810
The first line of it is 'any situation involving
a rate of change is described mathematically

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:15.950
in the language of calculus.'

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:22.040
It starts with the idea of a gradient then
works through differentiation, areas under

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:25.990
a curve, and integration.

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:34.090
And we've got circular functions, motion terms
and parametric equations, a visit to infinite

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:37.950
series and exponentials and logarithms.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:45.160
There's also differential equations, hyperbolic
functions and curves and surfaces.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:50.530
Some of the stuff I met at the end of high
school or at university and much of it I've

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:51.530
forgotten.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:55.229
I've slept on my integration so long I can
barely do the basics.

00:04:55.229 --> 00:05:01.251
So this book would do me well as a refresher,
it seems clear enough although from what I

00:05:01.251 --> 00:05:07.560
can see, it doesn't always explain too much
of the why, behind some of the definitions.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:13.220
It gives revision questions after every few
chapters with answers, which I like.

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:18.080
Someone who checked this out in the past,
maybe this year, or maybe 39 years ago, when

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.800
it was published, has made a lot of annotations
in pencil.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:23.770
I find these comforting.

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:31.090
Amongst all the seriousness of the math is
a human reminder and a source of company.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:36.620
It's a reminder as I tried to decipher each
example, that I'm not the only one who's been

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:37.620
here.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.759
They even left a little To Do List: 'Saturday
avo, read notes.'

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:47.770
This book would have just been published when
Terence read it, which is maybe why he picked

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:48.770
it.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:54.150
It is my opinion that when it comes to introductory
calculus textbooks, they all pretty much cover

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:55.960
the same sort of stuff.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:01.500
So if your goal is to learn calculus, then
the best book for you is probably just the

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.960
one that you have access to.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:09.639
Or it might be worth checking out a few sample
pages from a few options to see which of the

00:06:09.639 --> 00:06:11.770
explanations you prefer.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:18.130
On young Terence's reading list, there is
also Matrices and Vectors, Numbers, Inequalities

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:25.349
and Linear programming, Readings in mathematics,
and I found this picture of him reading Continued

00:06:25.349 --> 00:06:28.120
Fractions by Olds at age nine.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:34.510
Also on the list is the International Mathematics
Olympiad, a book of past problems.

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:39.639
And this is interesting because Terence would
go on to become the youngest competitor in

00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:40.639
this Olympiad.

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:42.330
At age 10.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:48.120
He won a bronze, silver and then a gold medal
at age 13, making him the youngest winner

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:50.350
of each of these categories.

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:57.140
And his two brothers, Nigel and Trevor have
also represented Australia at this competition.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:02.030
This next one is The Elements of Logic, and
it's got a pretty nice cover.

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:09.569
It goes through an introduction to logic as
a means to make deductive and inductive arguments,

00:07:09.569 --> 00:07:16.720
to understand truth functions, translating
words into symbols, fallacies, and applications

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:18.340
to philosophy.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:23.260
It says that logic can be intellectually rewarding
as knowledge for its own sake.

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:28.900
But it is also practical in helping us to
become more effective in recognising and avoiding

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:30.169
mistakes and reasoning.

00:07:30.169 --> 00:07:35.729
And a person who can recognise and avoid these
logical mistakes in reasoning, will be able

00:07:35.729 --> 00:07:42.099
to think more clearly and correctly and more
soundly and surely, regardless of what subjects

00:07:42.099 --> 00:07:43.620
they're thinking about.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:48.150
If you are wondering what logic has to do
with philosophy, then consider some of the

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:49.520
questions of philosophy.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.220
Like what is the difference between good and
evil?

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:55.370
Is it possible to have knowledge?

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:59.400
And can we ever prove that a thing is beautiful?

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:06.909
Well, logic also deals with very general questions,
like what good reasoning is, and the difference

00:08:06.909 --> 00:08:11.069
between correct and incorrect steps in thinking.

00:08:11.069 --> 00:08:15.159
And in both philosophy and logic, no experiments
are necessary.

00:08:15.159 --> 00:08:20.599
Before we can consider what is good and bad
reasoning, we need to consider what reasoning

00:08:20.599 --> 00:08:22.349
itself is.

00:08:22.349 --> 00:08:27.520
And it says here that reasoning is starting
with certain statements that you assume to

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:34.180
be true, then in thinking, moving on to a
consequence that you think follows from them.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:39.719
So what we're doing is making an inference,
constructing an argument and trying to give

00:08:39.719 --> 00:08:41.019
a proof.

00:08:41.019 --> 00:08:44.140
What we start with are called premises.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:47.630
And what we reach at the end is the conclusion.

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:53.010
And the book goes on to explore all the different
forms of reasoning.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:57.380
We start to see some symbols here with truth
tables.

00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:59.600
This line means NOT.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:06.720
And the symbol v means OR, so for example,
in the top line, if p is true, and q is true,

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:14.260
then p AND q has to be true, but NOT (p AND
q) would have to be false.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:23.600
NOT p is false, NOT q is false, NOT p AND
NOT q is false, and NOT p OR NOT q is also

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:24.600
false.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:30.310
And from there, we work up to the elementary
form of a valid argument.

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:37.240
Each line here represents a step of logic
that we can use to construct an argument.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:40.990
This first bit is read, if p then q.

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:45.560
So if p is true, therefore q is true.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:50.730
Kind of like when we're doing algebra, we
can replace something with an equivalent form.

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:54.070
We can do that here with logical expressions.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:57.500
These are a list of statements that are equivalent
to each other.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:03.470
So we can put it all together in an example
like this one here, we start with these first

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:11.200
three premises, we then make a series of logical
arguments, and end with forming a conclusion.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:17.120
This book was written in 1965 and there is
one application of logic that isn't mentioned,

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:20.580
but is now central to modern computing.

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:22.380
And that is logic gates.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:26.860
This part of the video is sponsored by Brilliant
and I'm going to work through their quiz on

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:29.220
logic gates to see how they work.

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:36.180
We see the symbols for AND, OR, and NOT, and
we can see what these basic gates would look

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:37.700
like.

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:44.010
AND outputs a one if both inputs are one,
OR outputs a one if at least one input is

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:48.639
one, NOT can take a one to a zero, or a zero
to a one.

00:10:48.639 --> 00:10:53.440
To combine these into a circuit means we can
chain a logical expression.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:11:02.910
The circuit here represents A AND NOT B, or
NOT A AND B.

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:07.770
We're asked what the output of the circuit
will be, and let's go through it.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:14.000
This first gate will output a one since both
inputs are one, that one would be combined

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:20.639
with a zero as input into the second AND,
and that would output a zero, since an AND

00:11:20.639 --> 00:11:25.120
requires that both inputs are one for the
output to be one.

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:29.200
Circuits, like logical expressions, can be
equivalent.

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:37.130
For example, these two circuits are equivalent
as two consecutive NOT gates have no effect.

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:41.380
And this is especially important for circuits
as we generally want to build ones that are

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:43.490
as small as possible.

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:48.510
Combining logic gates, we can perform all
sorts of operations, including things like

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:50.320
the addition of numbers.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:54.520
If you would like to continue this course
yourself, you can head to brilliant.org/Tibees.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:58.199
And that link will be down in the description
below.

